      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">Getting your invoice numbering right</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">This is where small businesses most often fall foul of the rules, and it is easily avoided. An invoice serial number must be consecutive, must be unique within the financial year, and may not exceed sixteen characters — letters, numbers, hyphens and slashes only. It must not restart mid-year or contain gaps, because gaps in a sequence are exactly what an audit looks for. A common workable pattern is a short prefix, the financial year, and a running count: INV/25-26/0001. Start a fresh sequence on the first of April each year and keep it unbroken, including for cancelled invoices, which should be marked cancelled rather than deleted from the sequence.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">Intra-state versus inter-state, and why the place of supply decides it</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">The single most common GST error on a manually prepared invoice is charging the wrong tax type, and it comes from confusing the customer's address with the place of supply. For goods, the place of supply is generally where the goods are delivered; for most services it is the recipient's location. If that place is in the same state as your registration, the tax splits into CGST and SGST at half the rate each. If it is a different state, you charge IGST at the full rate. The total the customer pays is identical either way, which is why the error goes unnoticed until returns are filed and the mismatch surfaces. Entering both states accurately lets the tool apply the split for you.</p>
      </div>
