        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">What must a GST tax invoice legally contain?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Under the CGST Rules an invoice needs: the supplier's name, address and GSTIN; a consecutive serial number for the financial year; the date of issue; the recipient's name, address and GSTIN where registered; the HSN or SAC code; a description, quantity and taxable value for each item; the rate and amount of CGST, SGST, IGST or cess; the place of supply; whether tax is payable on reverse charge; and a signature or digital signature. The generator prompts for each of these.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">When do I charge CGST and SGST instead of IGST?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">It depends on the place of supply. If the supplier and the place of supply are in the same state, the tax splits into CGST and SGST at half the total rate each. If they are in different states — or the supply is an export or to an SEZ — IGST applies at the full rate. Entering the correct state on both sides makes the tool apply the right split automatically.</p>
        </div>
